seg-497126fe23e9 = Navigation mode: an onboard filter

# text
# c1: [navigation]
# c2: [atmosphere]
seg-af7919ae046e = <c1/> flies a high-orbit or lunar-transfer craft and runs a nine-state extended Kalman filter (position, velocity, receiver clock bias and drift, a solar-radiation-pressure scale) on pseudoranges. Truth is integrated with point-mass gravity, J2, Sun and Moon third-body and radiation pressure with a cylindrical Earth shadow; the filter models gravity, J2 and the third bodies and estimates the radiation pressure. Pseudoranges come from the same link geometry and C/N0 threshold as the sweeps; their noise grows as the C/N0 falls; the medium's excess delay (with <c2/>) is added and not modelled by the filter. The report separates the part of the run inside the GNSS orbits (below { "20,000" } km altitude) from the part above, gives the longest gap with no measurement and the mean normalised estimation error squared (about { "3" } for a consistent position block). The Sun and Moon are low-precision circular stand-ins.

# attr:alt
seg-902c5c7da9aa = An onboard nine-state filter on a transfer from low orbit toward the Moon, from a real run of engine v{ $version } on scenarios/gnss-ssv-nav-lunar.toml ({ "289" } epochs, { "13" } points drawn). On a log axis, the true position error and the filter's own one-sigma bound against time since the start. Inside the GNSS orbits the position RMS is { "1.4" } m; above them it is { "90" } m, with the final error { "184" } m at { "193,330" } km altitude with { "1" } measurement. Mean normalised estimation error squared over the whole run is { "4.4" } (about { "3" } for a consistent position block). Truth and filter share their Sun and Moon stand-ins. MODELLED: a modelled study from stated inputs, not a measurement and not a service-volume claim for any system. Advisory: analysis output from a research and engineering tool, not type-approved navigation equipment and not a safety-of-life service.

